capstone 5.0.7
Upstream: https://github.com/capstone-engine/capstone, tag 5.0.7
  (archive https://github.com/capstone-engine/capstone/archive/refs/tags/5.0.7.tar.gz).
Taken from: third_party/capstone of another project of the author's at commit 49e811503d7ba5c9cff92160557df057c50a8ab3
  (that project's vendored copy; copied unchanged, nothing downloaded).
Source path: the archive's core (cs.c, Mapping.c, MCInst.c, MCInstrDesc.c, MCRegisterInfo.c, SStream.c, utils.c and
  their headers), include/capstone/, arch/X86 and arch/PowerPC; the other architectures keep only their *Module.h
  (cs.c includes them); the *_reduce tables dropped (CAPSTONE_X86_REDUCE off).

Build (CMakeLists.txt): target shsm_capstone, compiled with CAPSTONE_HAS_X86 only. The PowerPC decoder's sources are
vendored but compiled only with -DSHSM_CAPSTONE_PPC=ON (default OFF; adds arch/PowerPC/*.c and
CAPSTONE_HAS_POWERPC). The Python reference (tools/mwcc_trace/pe_disasm.py) uses the capstone 5.0.7 wheel, so the
same release gives the same instruction text.

Local patch (that project's, kept): arch/X86/X86IntelInstPrinter.c and X86ATTInstPrinter.c initialise `uint8_t encsize` to 0.
It was read uninitialised for the far-pointer operands of LCALL/LJMP (detail x86.encoding.imm_size: 0xCC in Debug
builds); 0 is what the Python capstone wheel reports.
